# StonkSmith, nightly.
#
# Install by pasting the schedule below into `crontab -e`. Do not run
# `crontab scripts/stonksmith.cron` unless you mean it: that replaces your
# whole crontab rather than adding to it. Keep a copy first either way --
#
#     crontab -l > ~/crontab.backup
#
# docs/scheduling.md is the record this file is derived from. It explains what
# each broker can do with nobody watching, and it is the thing to change first
# if a line here changes.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Why the schedule has the shape it has
#
#   One line per broker. The broker is a positional subcommand, so one process
#   runs one broker. There is no --all; N brokers is N lines.
#
#   Staggered, not fired together. `scraped_at` is stamped to the second and is
#   half a snapshot's key, so two runs landing in the same second collapse into
#   one snapshot. TSP is fast enough for this to matter.
#
#   The sheet goes after every broker has written, because it renders what the
#   databases hold at the moment it runs. The freshness check goes after the
#   sheet, for the same reason -- every entry above it changes what it reports
#   on.
#
#   --quiet on every broker run. Cron mails on output, so a run that says
#   nothing when it worked is a run that only mails when it did not.
#
#   --no-sheet on every broker run too, because every broker rewrites the whole
#   sheet when it finishes. Four brokers plus the sheet entry below is five full
#   rewrites of the same five tabs, four of which are then thrown away by the
#   next one. Staggered across a crontab that only wastes them; run back to back
#   -- which is what the launchd runner does -- it exhausts Sheets' write quota
#   per minute and the last one fails, which is the one that mattered. Observed:
#   `[429] Quota exceeded for quota metric 'Write requests'`.
#
#   It is a no-op on the ally entry, whose --from-prices path returns before it
#   would sync, and it is there anyway so all four broker lines read alike.
#
#   The sheet step is the exception, and not by oversight: `stonksmithdb` takes
#   no --quiet, and reports through its exit status instead -- 0 when the tabs
#   were rewritten, 1 when the sheet was unreachable, a tab refused, or a
#   broker database could not be read.
#
#   PATH is set because cron's is short and `uv` is usually not on it. `cd`
#   because the project is a uv workspace and `uv run` resolves it from the
#   working directory.
#
#   Check the PATH below actually contains uv before trusting a night of this:
#   `which uv` says where yours is, and uv's own installer puts it in
#   ~/.local/bin, which is *not* in the list. Add the directory it names, as an
#   absolute path -- cron does not expand `~` or `$HOME` in a PATH assignment,
#   so "$HOME/.local/bin" here is a literal directory that does not exist. A
#   PATH missing uv fails every entry identically, on the first night.
#
#   And check the `cd`, which names a checkout that is probably not yours.
#   ~/StonkSmith is where this file has to guess the repository is; there is no
#   way for it to know. A `cd` that fails takes its entry down with it, because
#   of the `&&` -- so a wrong path here fails every entry identically too, on
#   the same first night, for a reason that reads nothing like the PATH one. In
#   the repository, this rewrites them all to wherever you actually keep it:
#
#       sed -i '' "/^[0-9]/ s|cd ~/StonkSmith|cd $PWD|" scripts/stonksmith.cron
#
#   The /^[0-9]/ is load-bearing rather than tidy: it restricts the edit to the
#   entries, which are the only lines starting with a minute. Without it the
#   same command rewrites this comment too -- the line you are reading contains
#   the string it searches for -- and the instruction destroys itself on first
#   use. As written it is also safe to run twice.
#
#   (drop the '' on GNU sed). Both of these fail loudly rather than quietly,
#   which is the good version of wrong, and neither is worth discovering by
#   reading a week of cron mail.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Two absences that are deliberate
#
#   There is no `fidelity` line. Fidelity sits behind bot detection and 2FA, so
#   its scraper needs a human every time; SnapTrade covers the same accounts
#   unattended. Run both and the money lands on the Accounts tab twice, and the
#   total is wrong in the direction that looks good.
#
#   The `ally` line is not a scrape. Ally honours no restored session, so
#   --from-prices multiplies the units the last signed-in run recorded by
#   today's published close. The arithmetic is exact; the units are as old as
#   your last sign-in and go quietly wrong the moment a deposit lands. The run
#   says so on every account it values -- and --quiet means nobody will ever
#   see it. Re-run `--manual-login` after a deposit; this schedule cannot, and
#   will not ask.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The last entry asks whether anything has quietly stopped updating
#
#   `stonksmithdb stale` reads the databases and nothing else -- no login, no
#   Sheets, no network -- and exits 1 when an account's As Of is missing,
#   unreadable, or older than seven days. A day count widens the window:
#   `stonksmithdb stale 30` for a workspace whose brokers report monthly.
#
#   It will not catch the Ally problem described above, and it is worth being
#   clear about that rather than assuming otherwise. It reads each account's
#   As Of, and a repriced Ally mark carries the *price's* date -- today's. The
#   staleness there is in the units, which live somewhere this check does not
#   look. A broker that stops reporting altogether is what this entry finds.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Before the first night
#
#   Every line below is a no-op or a nightly failure until its broker is set
#   up. docs/scheduling.md has the checklist; the one that bites hardest is
#   Ally, which refuses and exits 1 against a database no signed-in run has
#   written. Run `stonksmith ally -M ally --manual-login` once first, or this
#   entry fails every night rather than once.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The times are local to the machine running cron.
#
#   What matters is that they fall after the 16:00 ET close and late enough
#   that TSP has published the day's share prices. 18:30 local satisfies both
#   on US Eastern, and on Pacific it lands at 21:30 ET, which is later still
#   and equally fine. Move them if your machine sits somewhere that makes
#   18:30 local earlier than about 18:30 Eastern.

PATH=/usr/local/bin:/usr/bin:/bin

30 18 * * 1-5  cd ~/StonkSmith && uv run stonksmith tsp -M tsp --no-sheet --quiet
35 18 * * 1-5  cd ~/StonkSmith && uv run stonksmith snaptrade -M snaptrade --no-sheet --quiet
40 18 * * 1-5  cd ~/StonkSmith && uv run stonksmith schwab529plan -M schwab529plan -id 1 --no-sheet --quiet
45 18 * * 1-5  cd ~/StonkSmith && uv run stonksmith ally -M ally --from-prices --no-sheet --quiet
50 18 * * 1-5  cd ~/StonkSmith && uv run stonksmithdb sheet
55 18 * * 1-5  cd ~/StonkSmith && uv run stonksmithdb stale
